WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
DuckTales, bless me bagpipes, when will that thrill-seeker Scrooge McDuck ever quit?

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.740
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.160
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.280
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.560
I think DuckTales is a TV series slash this Konami video game.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.200
You know, like these things are very close to some people because they grew up with the

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.160
show and stuff like that.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.020
Guys, what's the familiarity with the TV show DuckTales for,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.480
you know, for the two of you? I've only ever heard of it.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.560
I've heard of it a lot, but I've never seen any, any bit of it.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.900
When I, when I think of DuckTales, I think more about the indie band than I do about the show.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.080
I don't really know much about the show.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.000
Yeah, I feel like I just missed this.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.400
So I was actually surprised to learn that there were so

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.460
many other ducks besides Donald.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.620
I mean, I know Scrooge McDuck from the like Christmas Carol thing,

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.780
but I didn't think he was like an actual recurring character that would get a, you know, become a

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.100
playable video game character, let alone have a whole game centered around him collecting money.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:29.580
But now I'm, you know, I kind of did know that because I also have like a large collection of

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.240
Scrooge McDuck Dell comics from the 1960s.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.200
I just happened to like acquire them at a,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.160
like a garage sale one time and I was like, oh, cool.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.620
I'll just own these. But I never really

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.300
thought about it twice until now that we're playing DuckTales.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.060
Wow. But you knew Huey, Dewey and Louie, right?

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.700
That I did, but also that was from Kingdom Hearts.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.960
So again, like none of these things happen.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.300
Yeah. Well, in this game, it's more just like Huey, Louie and Louie.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:58.060
Right, right. Because Dewey just gets ripped off and he's just not on the main screen.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.080
Oh, I, you know what?

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.900
I didn't even notice. Yeah.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.380
Well, you know, another thing that I knew about this game going into it that I briefly

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:13.240
mentioned at the end of last week's episode was the song for the moon, that level that you go to,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.160
that is a very famous song that has been covered a lot of different times.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.920
And everybody always talks about it being like one of the best chiptune tracks ever.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.560
And rather than talk about, you know,

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.560
the music right off the bat, I just wanted to address a listener question that we got in via email.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:35.200
You can always email us by going to nostalgiacast.com and hitting the little form submission and it'll

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.440
send us an email that way.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.960
Anyway, we were asked, what are some of our favorite video game soundtracks,

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.520
retro or not?

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.700
So don't feel like you have to say NES games.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.860
Do you guys listen to video game music

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.240
music at all outside of playing video games?

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:53.760
Um, okay.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.540
So that's, that's like two questions.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:56.660
All right. Yes.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.620
Um, just answer the first one first.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.900
All right. The first one I'm going to cheat and kind

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:11.600
of say two, but, uh, one is, um, in terms of original music made for the game by a person that only made

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.420
music for the game.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.980
Um, it'd have to be Final Fantasy seven.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.480
All those songs are bangers.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:27.900
Um, and I still will sometimes listen to them on their own, but, uh, mostly, uh, they are like hip hop remixes.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:37.460
Um, and then like the one that I will listen to on their own, like all the time would be, uh, the, uh,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.660
Hotline Miami series, just like that entire soundtrack.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.400
It's all, it's from original artists,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:51.400
but, um, it's all music and it just sort of introduced me to, uh, the whole genre of synth wave.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.380
So, uh, that's a very important soundtrack to me.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.640
Yeah. I listened to actually quite a bit of video

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:00.340
game music, both games that I've played in games that I haven't.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.080
So it's hard for me to pick one

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.700
specific. I mean, I love, I also love anything that's like Nobuo Uematsu, particularly like

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.320
Final Fantasy eight soundtrack and stuff.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.560
And I mean, I listened to, there's a lot of like,

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:16.540
a lot of games like, like Ori and the Blind Forest, which I've never played, but I love the music and

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.520
tons of games where I just like listen to the soundtrack.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.680
But if I have to pick one, I, you know,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.020
it's maybe a little cheating because I don't know if it's necessarily my favorite, but I think it's like

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:30.360
the one that I like that is the most underrated or like the least appreciated is the original

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.700
Ratchet and Clank soundtrack by David Burgod, I believe.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:35.900
I don't know how you pronounce that,

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:43.580
but, uh, but it is just so like imaginative in the way that it's, it's like, it's like orchestral

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.640
and it sounds like Epic in that way, but it's also sort of like techno, like funky sounding.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.660
And it's just this like weird marriage of these two things that like, that fits the tone of like

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.940
exploring these other alien planets, but also being like adventurous so well that like, I really have

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.000
a, have an appreciation for those as well.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.960
Since we're all mentioning, uh, Nobuo and Final Fantasy

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:11.460
music, I thought it was also important to note that Nobuo's like music works outside of video games

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.840
is also very impressive and worth checking out.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.240
If you've only ever thought like,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.580
you know, Final Fantasy seven or Final Fantasy four or Final Fantasy six, like if you thought that

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.980
music was great, you should really see what he's done outside.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.060
And he's also done some cool projects

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.780
where he's taken that video game music and, you know, made it into like a rock band.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.520
He has a, the black mages is a group of his that, uh, does like rock versions of those songs.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:34.840
I think that's all really cool stuff.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.400
But in terms of favorite video game soundtrack, I'm going to go with a weird

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.680
one here because, uh, I'm sure everybody knows the ones that most commonly pop up on like

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.060
YouTube channels of just like, you know, 20 hours of Nintendo 64 music and stuff like that.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.780
For one that really like hits me in the right way is the Marvel versus Capcom two soundtrack.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.680
And that is because when you think about that video game and you think about the instructions

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:05.160
of like, you know, okay, Capcom's working on another street fighter esque video game where

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:11.380
you've, you know, you got Spider-Man punching the shit out of Ryu or, uh, Akuma taking down

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:16.100
Captain America, do you go to the composer and you're like, all right, we need the soundtrack

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.140
for that. And he comes back with just, you know, the hardest smooth jazz I've ever heard

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:28.040
in my life. Uh, these tracks are just, uh, you know, they don't belong in a fighting game

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:34.140
at all. And I listened to them regularly outside of playing Marvel versus Capcom two, just because

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.100
of the kind of feels that they provide.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.000
And then I have to remind myself like, oh yeah,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.020
but right now, like Wolverine's taking down Chun-Li, you know, it just doesn't match at

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.680
all. And I don't know how that soundtrack became the result.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:46.600
And I'm not just talking

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.220
about the title track too.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.000
Uh, I want to take you for a ride, which I know is like a meme

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.600
at this point in the fighting game community, but just the overall soundtrack too.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:01.640
If anybody just wants to like chill out for like a solid hour, every track on that is just very relaxing,

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.480
very, uh, you know, a wide eclectic amount of instruments involved.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.000
And, uh, I really enjoy

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.260
it. So I probably have played that album, you know, front to back, like 40 plus times.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:20.960
Nice. And I just wanted to, one last honorable mention, uh, shout out to Akiri Amalka and the,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:28.820
uh, industrial chill wave, uh, indie rock stylings of the Silent Hill soundtracks.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:59.000
Of course, we're not just here to talk about music and we will get to the DuckTales music,

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.920
but I think it's just important that we talk about DuckTales, the game first.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:08.660
Uh, you know, the, the, in DuckTales, you play as Scrooge McDuck, which is a weird choice in itself, but

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:15.040
you're Scrooge McDuck because he is traveling around the world and outer space in search of

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.160
five treasures to further increase his fortune.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.260
Uh, he has a, uh, I didn't know this, but he has a

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:26.620
rival Flintart Glumgold, who, uh, is also trying to become the world's richest duck.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:33.480
So throughout the game, you are acquiring all sorts of large sums of money and treasures and all these things add to

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:38.120
your, uh, you know, your net worth and your net worth is tracked throughout the game.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.220
And ultimately

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:45.560
the goal is collect the five treasures, take down, um, Glumgold and, uh, you know, become the richest

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.220
duck in the world. Sounds like a fun game, right?

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:49.820
Who doesn't want to be the richest duck?

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:57.480
Uh, I mean, uh, it is refreshing to just be like, there's, there's absolutely no honor to this.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:58.580
It's just, he wants money.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.640
So I can appreciate that.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.700
And Capcom's bringing in most of the Mega Man team

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.260
to do, uh, this game.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.980
So, uh, you know, I didn't get too many Mega Man vibes from this game, but it is

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:17.440
important to note that like a lot of the people who are very instrumental in creating Mega Man one and two,

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.380
uh, like Inafune are now making this DuckTales game.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.580
So clearly this isn't just some licensed cash

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.660
grab. They actually are assigning a, uh, a worthy team, a team that could have been working on Mega

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.800
Man three, but is instead now working on this DuckTales game.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.600
And I think the first obvious

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.760
place to spot that is the fact that you don't have to play the levels in a specific order.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.940
They took the Mega Man approach of a level select where all the levels are in front of you.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.700
You can directly go to

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:51.280
the moon or Transylvania, but you, it doesn't really matter what order you go in like Mega Man.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:54.640
There's no power up to acquire at the end of these stages.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.020
It just makes it to this way.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.160
Every time you boot up the game, you can decide which level you want to, uh, to tackle.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:01.340
I thought that was a neat approach.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.700
Yeah. I, I, I'm, I'm for that generally, as long as it doesn't ever get like

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:07.820
super unbalanced.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:12.400
Um, I'd say the only other thing that reminded me of Mega Man or gave me some vibe of

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.180
Mega Man was just the like aesthetic.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.840
It just got this very clean and like,

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.220
uh, it looks good.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:25.380
The game, the game looks, uh, like the art looks good compared to a lot of other

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:29.480
NES games, particularly platformers like this, uh, around this time.

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:36.560
The, the Mega Man vibe, uh, was most prominent to me, uh, when you're sort of going between,

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:42.220
uh, screens, when it's not like the smooth scrolling and, and you're sort of going into

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:49.780
the, the, the boss part, or if you're kind of going in from a, like a vertical fall through a

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:54.460
whole thing, it does that sort of stutter, um, slow down.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:57.320
Um, that is like always reminded me of

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.400
going into the boss sections for Mega Man.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.880
But other than that, I would not have thought this,

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:04.800
uh, but, um, yeah.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.660
And then it's got that whole, uh, you know, choose your own adventure,

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.020
like, or go, go to whatever level you want.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.300
And I think that the difference here is that like with,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.440
with Mega Man, um, there were, there's like an optimal route.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:20.660
Whereas with this, like,

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.120
there's definitely some parts where like, it's going to make you go back and get something in

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:30.440
another level, but it's not, it's not something that you have to play over and over to find like,

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.260
oh, this boss would be a lot easier if I had this.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:35.760
Um, so it's just a lot more free form.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.580
Yeah. And I think in a game that approaches itself, you know, as a duck tales, like you're

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:43.280
playing a screwed McDuck, it's kind of supposed to be kid friendly.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:44.760
I think that is the right

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.740
approach to take like all the things that worked about Mega Man and just scale down the difficulty

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.560
a little bit, the intricacies, you know, a little bit.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.660
There's a whole thing about each level.

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:57.440
You're just supposed to collect the treasure, right?

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.740
Like that's the end goal for each level is to beat

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:05.060
the boss by bonking him on the head, like four times and then collecting that treasure.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.120
And that's what brings you back to the main menu screen.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.460
But the game also opts to have like, there's the path

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:11.420
to get to the treasure.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:15.700
And then there's the money path, like all these extra places you can go to

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:21.360
and things to do that either have fun secrets or, uh, you know, are turned out to be dead ends,

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:25.840
which they're pretty good about not doing dead ends in that sense, but just like extra ways to

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:27.680
increase your score and stuff like that.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.500
So that if you did want to just, you know, stumble upon

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:36.200
the boss or get to the boss in the quickest way, there's that path, but then there's also

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:39.680
these branching paths of different ways to do that.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.560
And I thought that was also a, a neat approach in the,

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:48.220
in the level design because you didn't have to just, you know, it was clear what your goal was,

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:53.400
right? Of like getting to the end of the stage to, you know, fight the boss or whatever, but you also

00:12:53.400 --> 00:13:00.060
didn't have to, um, you didn't have to collect a certain amount of treasure or, you know, hit a certain

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.720
amount of points to unlock a gate.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.440
You know, it wasn't gated or off or anything until you did

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:05.540
certain things.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.560
The only question I have then is why a timer?

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.260
It doesn't really make any sense to

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:15.520
have this timer. And I, you know, I'm thinking back to like, you know, a game that I believe we

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:20.820
recorded in an alternate universe, uh, Sonic the Hedgehog, where, uh, you know, versions of,

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:26.260
versions of us probably had complained about, you know, the timer for the same exact reasons.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:31.740
You know, you've created this level where you can explore and take on different routes and there's,

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:37.460
uh, you know, multiple branching paths and stuff, but the timer actually limits you because if you,

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:42.160
you know, fool around too much with collecting the money, then it's, you know, uh, you get,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:46.480
you lose a life and have to restart from like, uh, a self-assigned checkpoint.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:47.800
So it doesn't,

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.220
you know, it doesn't really help in the whole like exploration category here.

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.300
I'm a little confused

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:55.720
by the timer. They do give you an ample amount of time, so I'm not going to complain about it too

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:02.080
much, but in terms of like fighting exploration and fun versus straightforwardness, I felt like they,

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:07.060
you know, they conflicted themselves there because there is a way to play the game and get to the

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:10.620
boss super quick and do it like speed run style or just like, you know, I beat this before.

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:11.580
I just want to beat it again.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.940
And then there's the more fun path of like collecting all the possible money

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.060
you can. Yeah.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:21.180
I'm generally, I'm generally not a fan of timers and like in a game that's not

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:23.980
outright trying to be about speed.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:25.960
So like, it is a little weird.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:28.140
I mean, I get, I get the argument

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:31.340
that it's like, Oh, well, they're trying to like make it feel a little more stressful.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.960
Give you a, put a little pressure on you when you're exploring to like have to find, to remember to find your way

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:37.220
out. I get that.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:38.460
I don't think it's needed here.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.940
So it's like the timer is weird, but like you said,

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.120
the timer is a, it's a very long timer.

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:46.480
So, so it rarely ended up becoming a problem,

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.220
but it is a little bit of an annoyance.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.920
Yeah. I never, I barely even noticed it because you have

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:56.500
so much time, even though I was also exploring and going to the nooks and crannies.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.000
So I didn't really have a problem with it.

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:03.340
It is still extraneous because like, again, there's, there's no reason for

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:06.480
it to exist other than to reward fast play.

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.780
But then what are, why are you pushing, why are you

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:11.060
priding them to explore then?

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:15.140
It's, it's, it's counterintuitive design, but I don't see it as frustrating.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:16.020
Yeah.

